---
/**
 * START HERE PAGE
 * ===============
 * The path from "I have never used GitHub" to "my change is live on the club
 * website". Every learning track points here first, because all of them
 * assume you can get code in and out of GitHub.
 *
 * The steps are written as data in STEPS below, then drawn once. To change the
 * wording, edit the text there — you should not need to touch the markup.
 */

import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';

import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import HexGrid from '../components/HexGrid.astro';
import Prompt from '../components/Prompt.astro';
import PullRequestDiagram from '../components/PullRequestDiagram.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/href';
import { CLUB } from '../config/site';

/**
 * The onboarding steps, in order.
 *
 * `where` is a promise to the reader about what they need to get it done:
 *   'school' - works fine on a school computer during a meeting
 *   'home'   - needs a document or an adult, so do it outside the meeting
 *   'either' - works anywhere you have a browser
 */
const STEPS = [
  {
    n: 1,
    title: 'Make a GitHub account',
    where: 'school',
    time: '5 minutes',
    link: { label: 'github.com/signup', url: 'https://github.com/signup' },
    body: [
      'GitHub is where the club keeps its code. It is free, and it is the same site professional developers use every day.',
      'You must be at least 13 to have an account — that is GitHub’s rule, not ours.',
    ],
    warning:
      'Choose your username carefully. It is public, it is how you will be credited on this website, and it is awkward to change later. Pick something you would be happy showing a future employer — and do not use your full name, your student ID, or your birth year.',
  },
  {
    n: 2,
    title: 'Join the Novato High School organization',
    where: 'school',
    time: '2 minutes, then wait for the invite',
    body: [
      'Our projects live in a shared GitHub organization. Being a member means you can be assigned tasks and review other people’s work.',
      'You cannot add yourself — an owner has to invite you. Bring your GitHub username to a meeting, and you will get an email invitation to accept.',
    ],
  },
  {
    n: 3,
    title: 'Apply for GitHub Education',
    where: 'home',
    time: '10 minutes, then a few days to hear back',
    link: {
      label: 'Apply for GitHub Education',
      url: 'https://github.com/settings/education/benefits',
    },
    body: [
      'GitHub gives enrolled students a pile of professional developer tools for free. It is genuinely worth doing, and it is yours to keep as long as you are a student.',
      'You need to prove you are enrolled. A school ID showing a current date, a class schedule, a transcript, or an enrollment letter all count.',
    ],
    warning:
      'Do this one at home or with a teacher — you will be photographing a school document, and it is easier when you are not rushing at lunch.',
  },
  {
    n: 4,
    title: 'Find the story',
    where: 'either',
    time: '5 minutes',
    action: 'play',
    body: [
      'There is a text adventure hidden in the terminal on the front page of this site. It is an alternative to fighting the firewall knight, and it is the thing you are about to add to — so play it first. You cannot write a room for a game you have not been inside.',
      'Nobody is going to tell you the way in. Open the terminal and type cat .secrets; whoever was here before you left notes, and the notes are the trail.',
    ],
  },
  {
    n: 5,
    title: 'Write a scene',
    where: 'school',
    time: '15 minutes',
    action: 'write-scene',
    body: [
      'Every room in that story is one Markdown file in src/content/story/, and adding a room means adding one file. You write where it hangs off, what the reader types to get there, and what they find.',
      'You do not touch anybody else’s file, which is deliberate: ten people can each add a scene in the same meeting and none of you will collide.',
    ],
    warning:
      'Your scene must either lead somewhere or end the story. If it does nothing, the automatic check will say so in plain English and tell you which of the two to add — that is the check doing its job, not you failing.',
  },
  {
    n: 6,
    title: 'Turn it into a pull request',
    where: 'school',
    time: '5 minutes, then wait for a review',
    action: 'pull-request',
    body: [
      'Your scene branches the story. Saving it branches the code — same word, very nearly the same idea. A branch is your own copy of the project where your change lives until somebody has looked at it.',
      'A pull request is you saying: here is my branch, please put it in the real one. A check builds the whole site to make sure nothing broke, somebody reads it, and then it is live.',
    ],
    warning:
      'When GitHub asks how to save, choose "Create a new branch and start a pull request". It may offer to commit straight to main instead — that option will be refused, because nobody pushes to main here, including the advisor.',
  },
  {
    n: 7,
    title: 'Get a real workspace',
    where: 'either',
    time: '5 minutes to start it up',
    action: 'codespace',
    body: [
      'When you want to change how the site looks or works — not just its words — you need to actually run it. A Codespace is a full computer in the cloud, opened from a browser tab, with the site running live next to your code.',
      'We have already written the setup, so it builds itself. Your GitHub account includes a monthly allowance of free Codespace time; stop it when you are done and it costs you nothing.',
    ],
  },
];

/**
 * The template a new scene file starts with.
 *
 * GitHub lets us pre-fill a new file through the URL, so the first thing a
 * contributor sees is a working example with their cursor in it rather than
 * an empty box and a guess about the format. Every field is explained in
 * src/content.config.ts, and the automatic check quotes that file by name if
 * anything is missing.
 */
const SCENE_TEMPLATE = `---
# The scene your new one hangs off. This has to match another file's name
# in src/content/story/, without the .md on the end.
from: sub-level

# What the reader types to get here. One or two words.
choice: knock

# true if your scene ENDS the story and lets the reader into the site.
# If you set this to false, something else has to follow your scene.
ending: true

# Your GitHub username, so you get the credit. Username only, never a name.
author: your-github-username
---

Write what the reader finds here. Keep the lines short - about 68
characters - because this gets printed into a terminal.
`;

// The repository everything on this page points at.
const REPO_URL = `${CLUB.githubOrg}/Dev-Club`;

/**
 * Links straight into GitHub's new-file editor, with the filename box and the
 * file itself already filled in.
 *
 * This is the plain GitHub website rather than one of the browser editors,
 * because it is the lightest thing that works and because its save dialog is
 * the whole branch-and-pull-request flow as a form — which is exactly what
 * step 6 is trying to teach.
 */
const NEW_SCENE_URL =
  `${REPO_URL}/new/main/src/content/story` +
  `?filename=my-scene.md&value=${encodeURIComponent(SCENE_TEMPLATE)}`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Start here"
  description="How to go from never having used GitHub to having your first change live on the Dev Club website."
>
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-edge">
    <HexGrid class="opacity-50" />
    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 py-16">
      <Prompt command="dev-club onboard --new-member" />
      <h1 class="mt-4 font-mono text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        Start here
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Seven steps from never having touched GitHub to having your name &mdash;
        well, your username &mdash; on something real: a room in the text
        adventure hidden on this site, written by you. Most people get through
        steps 4 to 6 in a single meeting.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-14">
    <ol class="space-y-5">
      {
        STEPS.map((step) => (
          <li class="rounded-lg border border-edge bg-surface p-6">
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-2">
              {/* The step number, styled like a line number in an editor. */}
              <span class="font-mono text-2xl font-bold text-gold">
                {String(step.n).padStart(2, '0')}
              </span>
              <h2 class="font-mono text-lg font-semibold">{step.title}</h2>

              <span class="ml-auto flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs">
                <span
                  class:list={[
                    'rounded-full border px-2 py-0.5',
                    step.where === 'school' && 'border-acid/40 text-acid',
                    step.where === 'home' && 'border-cyan/40 text-cyan',
                    step.where === 'either' && 'border-edge text-muted',
                  ]}
                >
                  {step.where === 'school' && 'do it at a meeting'}
                  {step.where === 'home' && 'do it at home'}
                  {step.where === 'either' && 'do it anywhere'}
                </span>
                <span class="text-muted">{step.time}</span>
              </span>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
              {step.body.map((paragraph) => (
                <p>{paragraph}</p>
              ))}
            </div>

            {/* Anything the reader should know BEFORE doing the step. */}
            {step.warning && (
              <p class="mt-4 flex gap-2.5 rounded border border-gold/30 bg-gold/5 p-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-bone">
                <Icon
                  name="lucide:triangle-alert"
                  class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-gold"
                />
                <span>{step.warning}</span>
              </p>
            )}

            {/* ----------------------------------------------------------
                The buttons for the steps that have something to press.

                Anything that leaves this site opens in a NEW TAB, so the
                instructions are still here when the reader comes back. Every
                such link is marked with the same small arrow icon, which is
                what that icon means everywhere else on this page.
            ---------------------------------------------------------- */}
            {step.action === 'play' && (
              <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
                <a
                  href={`${href('/')}?boot`}
                  class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded border border-gold bg-gold px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs font-medium text-ink transition-colors hover:bg-transparent hover:text-gold"
                >
                  <Icon name="lucide:terminal" class="h-3.5 w-3.5" />
                  Open the terminal
                </a>
              </div>
            )}

            {step.action === 'write-scene' && (
              <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
                <a
                  href={NEW_SCENE_URL}
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener"
                  class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded border border-gold bg-gold px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs font-medium text-ink transition-colors hover:bg-transparent hover:text-gold"
                >
                  <Icon name="lucide:pencil" class="h-3.5 w-3.5" />
                  Write a scene on GitHub
                  <Icon name="lucide:external-link" class="h-3 w-3 opacity-70" />
                </a>
                <a
                  href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/src/content/story`}
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener"
                  class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded border border-edge px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-bone transition-colors hover:border-gold hover:text-gold"
                >
                  Read the existing scenes
                  <Icon name="lucide:external-link" class="h-3 w-3 opacity-70" />
                </a>
              </div>
            )}

            {/* Step 6 is the one about branches, so it gets the picture. */}
            {step.action === 'pull-request' && (
              <PullRequestDiagram class="mt-6" />
            )}

            {step.action === 'codespace' && (
              <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
                <a
                  href={`https://codespaces.new/Novato-High-School/Dev-Club?quickstart=1`}
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener"
                  class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded border border-gold bg-gold px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs font-medium text-ink transition-colors hover:bg-transparent hover:text-gold"
                >
                  <Icon name="lucide:cloud" class="h-3.5 w-3.5" />
                  Open a Codespace
                  <Icon name="lucide:external-link" class="h-3 w-3 opacity-70" />
                </a>
                <a
                  href={href('/build')}
                  class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded border border-edge px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-bone transition-colors hover:border-gold hover:text-gold"
                >
                  Find something to work on
                </a>
              </div>
            )}

            {step.link && (
              <a
                href={step.link.url}
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener"
                class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 font-mono text-xs text-cyan transition-colors hover:text-gold"
              >
                <Icon name="lucide:external-link" class="h-3.5 w-3.5" />
                {step.link.label}
              </a>
            )}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    {/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
        THE TWO WAYS TO WORK, compared side by side.

        Deliberately says nothing about whether either of these is reachable
        from the school network. Some schools filter developer sites and some
        do not, we cannot test every network a member will sit on, and a
        confident claim that turns out to be wrong is worse than no claim -
        a student who was promised it works concludes THEY broke it. So the
        page describes what each tool is for and says what to do when a page
        does not load, which is true everywhere.
    ------------------------------------------------------------------ */}
    <div class="mt-14">
      <Prompt command="which editor" />
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-mono text-2xl font-semibold">
        Two ways to work
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Pick by what you are doing, not by which sounds more impressive. Almost
        everything a new member does is the one on the left.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-7 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-edge bg-surface p-6">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <Icon name="lucide:pencil" class="h-5 w-5 text-gold" />
            <h3 class="font-mono text-base font-semibold">The GitHub website</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Editing a file straight on github.com. No setup, nothing to install,
            and the save dialog walks you through making a branch and opening a
            pull request.
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-1.5 font-mono text-xs text-muted">
            <li><span class="text-acid">+</span> opens instantly</li>
            <li><span class="text-acid">+</span> perfect for Markdown and text</li>
            <li><span class="text-acid">+</span> teaches the branch and review flow</li>
            <li>
              <span class="text-gold">&minus;</span> cannot run the site or use a
              terminal
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-lg border border-edge bg-surface p-6">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <Icon name="lucide:cloud" class="h-5 w-5 text-gold" />
            <h3 class="font-mono text-base font-semibold">Codespaces</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            A whole computer in a browser tab, with the site running live beside
            your code. For changing how the site works, not just what it says.
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-1.5 font-mono text-xs text-muted">
            <li><span class="text-acid">+</span> real terminal, runs the site</li>
            <li><span class="text-acid">+</span> set up for you already</li>
            <li>
              <span class="text-gold">&minus;</span> a few minutes to start; stop
              it when done
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="text-gold">&minus;</span> uses part of your monthly
              free allowance
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* The honest note about networks, in place of a promise we cannot keep. */}
      <p class="mt-6 flex gap-2.5 rounded border border-edge p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        <Icon name="lucide:wifi-off" class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-muted" />
        <span>
          If one of these opens to a blank or half-loaded page, that is almost
          always the network blocking it rather than anything you did. School
          and workplace networks filter sites they do not recognise. Try it on a
          different network before you spend any time debugging it, and tell us
          which one failed &mdash; that is useful to know.
        </span>
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
        WHERE TO GO NEXT
    ------------------------------------------------------------------ */}
    <div class="mt-14 rounded-lg border border-dashed border-edge p-6">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        <span class="font-mono text-gold">//</span> Stuck on any of this? That is
        expected, and it is what meetings are for. Bring the thing that is not
        working &mdash; a screenshot of the error is plenty.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a
          href={href('/learn')}
          class="rounded border border-edge px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-bone transition-colors hover:border-gold hover:text-gold"
        >
          Pick a learning track
        </a>
        <a
          href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`}
          class="rounded border border-edge px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-bone transition-colors hover:border-gold hover:text-gold"
        >
          Read CONTRIBUTING.md
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
